ModelControl u_tNoise onUse whenOdometry modelodometry’s pose change: turn, drive, turneach of the three partsencoders exist; filtering after the moveVelocity modelcommanded speeds v, \omega for a time step \Delta tthe speeds v, \omega and an extra end turn \gammano encoders, or predicting before the move (planning)Map-consistent modeleither of the above, plus a mapas above; poses inside obstacles get 0a map is known and steps are small

The command of the opening, 0.5 m/s and 1 rad/s for 1 s, does not lead to the one pose (2.249, 1.409) but to a cloud around it, because wheels slip and motors lag; a motion model (G-2319)Motion model p(\mathbf x_t \mid u_t, \mathbf x_{t-1}): The probability distribution of a robot’s pose after a move, given its pose before and the control (command or odometry); it describes how uncertain motion is, so a filter can score possible end poses or draw them at random. Explained in §2.2 The motion model and its density. p(\mathbf x_t \mid u_t, \mathbf x_{t-1}), the density of the end pose \mathbf x_t given the control u_t and the start pose \mathbf x_{t-1}, describes that cloud; its values are densities, so only areas are probabilities (0.048 for the band 1.09 to 1.11 m) and a density can exceed 1.
Wheel odometry (G-2321)Wheel odometry: Working out a robot’s pose by adding up the wheel motion its encoders measure: ticks to wheel distances, to the robot’s move and turn, to a new pose; accurate over short distances but it drifts, because every error stays in the sum. Explained in §3.1 From encoder ticks to a new pose. turns encoder ticks into wheel distances, the robot’s move and a new pose; it drifts because each error stays in the running sum, and a 1 percent wrong wheel radius already puts the robot 2.49 m off after 10 m, so robots need calibration.
The odometry model splits each reported move into turn, drive, turn and puts noise on each, because that matches real robots better than one Gaussian on the end pose; its standard deviations (G-1870)Standard deviation of a random variable: The square root of a random variable’s variance, in the units of X; it says how far values typically fall from the expected value. Explained in: Random Variables as Functions, Expected Value and Variance, §4.1 Variance term by term, the typical sizes of the noise, grow with the motion, because bigger motions slip more.
Scoring a pose multiplies three normal densities (76.7 for a close pose, 8.7 \times 10^{-7} for a far one), so filters can compare poses on a grid.
Sampling (G-2326)Sampling (from a distribution): Drawing random values so that, over many draws, each value comes up as often as a given distribution says; it turns a motion model into a cloud of possible poses for particle filters. Explained in §5.1 Why sample instead of scoring. draws noisy turn, drive, turn and moves the robot, so a particle filter gets a cloud of end poses without scoring a grid of a million cells.
Large rotation noise bends the cloud into a banana, because a wrong turn sends the robot the same distance in a wrong direction; with no readings the cloud keeps growing.
The velocity model drives an exact arc about the ICC, at the turning radius R, the linear velocity divided by the angular velocity, to the robot’s left; it needs a third noise \gamma, an extra end turn, because two noisy speeds alone tie the end heading to the end position.
A map rules out poses inside obstacles by rejecting samples there, which is safe only for small steps, because the check looks at where a sample ends, not at the path to it.

The robot can now say how far a move blurs its pose, score any end pose and draw the cloud. Still missing: a reading only means something against a map of where things are, which is maps and landmarksExplained in: Maps and Landmarks, §1. Overview.
