The cup task is solved by four actions in a fixed order, move start table, pick-up table, move table door, put-down door, which breadth-first forward search finds after taking 8 states, because each state records the cup and the hand that a grid cell leaves out; the two moves are routes of 0.707 m and 2.707 m, the same 3.414 m as the direct route to the door.
A state (G-2602)State (task planning): The set of facts that are true at one moment, every other fact being false, such as {robot-at start, cup-at table, hand-empty}; it records the objects and the robot’s hand, which a grid cell cannot. Explained in §2.1 Facts and states. is the set of true facts (G-2601)Fact (task planning): A statement about the task that is either true or false, such as robot-at table or holding-cup; facts are what a planner’s states, actions and goals are made of. Explained in §2.1 Facts and states.; 8 facts allow 256 sets but only 12 can happen, so we never list states and let the planner make them as it goes.
A STRIPS action (G-2605)STRIPS action: An action described by three lists of facts, its precondition, add list and delete list, as in the STRIPS planner of the Shakey robot; one such description replaces a table of the action’s result in every state. Explained in §3.1 Precondition, add list and delete list. lists its precondition (G-2606)Precondition: The facts that must be true for an action to be done, such as robot-at table, cup-at table and hand-empty for picking up the cup; the planner tries an action only in states that hold all of them. Explained in §3.1 Precondition, add list and delete list., add list (G-2607)Add list: The facts an action makes true, such as holding-cup for picking up the cup; applying the action puts them into the state. Explained in §3.1 Precondition, add list and delete list. and delete list (G-2608)Delete list: The facts an action makes false, such as cup-at table and hand-empty for picking up the cup; applying the action takes them out of the state. Explained in §3.1 Precondition, add list and delete list., so applying it is “take out the delete list, put in the add list”, as pick-up table turns S1 into S3.
STRIPS keeps every fact that no list mentions as it was, so the 66 “stays the same” lines of the frame problem (G-2610)Frame problem: The difficulty that a logical description of an action must also state everything the action leaves unchanged, one statement per action and fact (66 of 96 lines in the cup task); STRIPS avoids it with the rule that a fact on neither list keeps its value. Explained in §3.3 Why lists of changes: the frame problem. are never written.
PDDL (G-2612)PDDL (Planning Domain Definition Language): The standard language for writing a planning task, defined in 1998 for the International Planning Competition: actions with parameters, preconditions and effects, in a domain file and a problem file; a task written in it can be handed to any planner that reads PDDL. Explained in §4.1 One action with parameters. writes each action once with parameters (G-2614)Parameter (PDDL action): A blank in an action, written with a question mark such as ?from and ?to in move ?from ?to, that the planner fills with objects; one action with parameters replaces many STRIPS actions (6 moves for 3 places, 90 for 10). Explained in §4.1 One action with parameters., and splits the task into a domain file (G-2615)Domain file (PDDL): The PDDL file that holds the parts of a task family that never change: its predicates and its actions; it is written once and reused for every problem in that family. Explained in §4.2 The domain file. and a problem file (G-2616)Problem file (PDDL): The PDDL file that holds one task: its objects, its start state and its goal; a new task needs only a new problem file for the same domain file. Explained in §4.3 The problem file., so a new task needs only a new problem file and any planner can solve it.
Forward search (G-2617)Forward search (progression): Planning by starting at the start state, applying every applicable action to make the next states, and searching the resulting graph of states until one meets the goal; any graph search, such as breadth-first search or A*, can run it. Explained in §5.1 The state graph, built while searching. is graph search on states made from the action lists; breadth-first search returns the plan with the fewest actions, and A* with an admissible heuristic returns the same plan after taking 5 states instead of 8, because its guess steers it along the plan.

The robot can now turn a task with objects into an ordered list of actions, and each move into a route of grid cells. Still missing: the route moves a point from cell to cell, while the real robot is a body with a size and a heading. Configuration spaceConfiguration space (C-space): The set of all configurations of a robot, one point per placement of the whole robot, such as a box of (x, y, \theta) whose top and bottom faces are the same headings for a floor robot; motion planning looks for paths through it. Explained in: Configuration Space and Degrees of Freedom, §2.2 The configuration space of the floor robot describes every placement of the whole robot with a few numbers.
